SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 305 | Thursday, March 5, 2026

Good morning.

1) Dry van spot rates rise for a third straight week
National average dry van spot at $2.14 a mile excluding fuel, up nine cents since mid-February. Carriers have been exiting for two years; capacity is finally tight enough that a modest demand bump moves the price. Contract rates lag spot by about six months, so the shipper budgets set in January will look optimistic by summer.

2) The EU's packaging rule starts to bite
The Packaging and Packaging Waste Regulation's first obligations apply from August: minimum recycled content in plastic packaging and a ban on several single-use formats in food service. US exporters of packaged food to the EU are the ones scrambling. Compliance consultants have doubled their prices, which is how you know a rule is real.

3) Mexico announces a $12B rail freight plan
Six new corridors, mostly connecting the Bajio industrial belt to the Gulf ports and to Laredo. Whether it gets built is a different question; the last two plans did not. What is different this time is that the automakers are co-funding the Bajio segment.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 56%. Share of US warehouses in a survey out today that report using at least one autonomous mobile robot, up from 39% two years ago. The median site has eleven robots and one person who knows how to reset them.

That's the morning. Reply with tips.

